# Overview
#
# Each phrase states an obligation without saying how strong it is, so
# the reader cannot tell a requirement from advice (`needs to`, `is
# expected to`, `is recommended`). The fix writes `must` for a
# requirement and `should` for advice, the grades the technical-writing
# skill defines, or a bare command for a step. When the subject is the
# system, the fix states what it does in the present tense (`the server
# needs to listen` becomes `the server listens`). The rule sits at
# Recommended, because some hits describe a situation or a hypothetical,
# where neither grade fits.
#
# Expression
#
# `need to` carries a guard on the word before it, and `neg` lists the
# words that the guard skips. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter
# consumes the word before `need` and narrows the finding back to the
# phrase. A second guard skips `know` after the phrase. The guard on
# `to` also drops `the hosts that the client connects to need to support
# TLS`, an obligation that the rule misses.
#
# `going to need to` has a branch of its own, because the guard on `to`
# skips it (`you are going to need to restart`). Its guard skips the
# words of `neg` before `going`, so a negation still passes (`you are
# not going to need to restart`). `neg` lists `aren't`, `isn't`,
# `wasn't`, and `weren't` for this branch (`you aren't going to need to
# restart`). The guard needs a word to consume, so a sentence that opens
# with `Going to need to` passes.
#
# `it is necessary to`, `it is essential to`, `it is critical to`, and
# `it is best to` hide the grade the same way. So do `are advised to`
# and `are encouraged to`, with or without an adverb before `advised` or
# `encouraged` (`users are strongly encouraged to`). An adverb is a word
# that describes a verb, an adjective, or a whole sentence, such as
# `safely` or `also`.
#
# After `will`, `may`, or `might`, `want to` counts with any subject
# (`users will want to pin the version`). The contraction counts after
# `I`, `you`, `we`, `they`, `he`, `she`, and `it` (`they'll want to pin
# the version`).
#
# False Positives
#
# The guard before the phrase skips a word that states that no
# obligation exists, such as a negation (`you do not need to restart`,
# `you won't need to restart`). It skips `only` and `just`, which state
# what suffices (`you only need to set the flag once`, `we just need to
# hand back control`). It also skips `rarely`, which states that a step
# is rare (`you rarely need to annotate types`). In the measure.py
# corpus, `just` or `rarely` sits before `need to` on 9 lines, and each
# of the 9 states what suffices or that a step is rare.
#
# The guard skips `to`, because `to need to` describes a situation
# instead of a duty (`it is rare to need to call the method directly`,
# `we do not want to need to update the test`). In the measure.py
# corpus, `to` sits before `need to` on 1 line, which states that a step
# is rare.
#
# A modal verb is a helping verb that marks possibility, ability, or
# obligation, such as `can`, `might`, or `must`. The guard skips a modal
# verb that marks a possibility, such as `might` or `could` (`you might
# need to restart`). The sentence then describes a case that might not
# arise.
#
# The guard also skips a determiner (a word that introduces a noun, such
# as `the`, `a`, or `this`), where `need` is a noun (`the need to
# scale`). `need to know` passes, because `everything you need to know`
# states no duty. `you want to` is left out, because `If you want to X,
# do Y` is a fork the technical-writing skill allows.
#
# `If you need to X, do Y` still counts, because Weir cannot anchor a
# branch to the start of a sentence, and a branch that catches `You need
# to restart` also catches `if you need to`. The condition-first form of
# the technical-writing skill reads better anyway (`To X, do Y`), so the
# message names it.
#
# `will want to` after a subject other than `you` still fires where it
# predicts what people want instead of advising the reader (`gui users
# will want to create their own types`, `it's more likely we'll want to
# move code definitions`). `must` or `should` changes the meaning there,
# and the rule accepts that misfire.
#
# Related Rules
#
# `it is important to` counts here except before the verbs that the
# Required ProseFiller reports (`it is important to note`). The Optional
# ProsePassive lists `recommended`, `advised`, and `encouraged` as
# stative words, ones that name a state, so `it is recommended that` and
# `are encouraged to` each draw one finding, from this rule.
#
# ProseFutureTense skips `will want to` and `will need to`, so only this
# rule reports them. The guard on `know` skips `will need to know` here,
# so ProseFutureTense reports that phrase.
#
# The Recommended ProseObligation reports `need to` and the other
# phrases that read as advice. The Optional ProseObligation reports `has
# to` and `have to`, which often state a reason or a burden instead.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 830 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 15 of 20 sampled
# hits stated obligations that `must`, `should`, the present tense, or a
# `To` opener states more clearly. Of the five misfires, three described
# a situation (`nodes that need to be upgraded to a Kubernetes
# release`), and one predicted what the reader wants (`Often, you'll
# want to combine two existing strings`). One quoted license text
# (`shall mean the work of authorship`).
expr need [need, needs]
expr neg [not, never, don't, doesn't, didn't, won't, wouldn't, shouldn't, aren't, isn't, wasn't, weren't, only, just, rarely, to, might, may, would, could, DET]
expr wrap [expected, required, supposed]
expr verbs [note, remember, understand, mention, realize, recognize]
expr main [
    <(!@neg @need to !know), (@need to)>,
    <(!@neg going to @need to !know), (@need to)>,
    ([(it is), it's] [necessary, essential, critical, best] to),
    ([is, are] [advised, encouraged] to),
    ([is, are] ADV [advised, encouraged] to),
    ([is, are] @wrap to),
    ([is, are] recommended),
    <([(it is), it's] important to !@verbs), ([(it is), it's] important to)>,
    (it is advisable),
    (it is a good idea),
    ([I'll, you'll, we'll, they'll, he'll, she'll, it'll] want to),
    ([will, may, might] want to),
    (be sure to),
    (make sure to),
    (ought to),
    (must try to),
    shall
]
let message "Vague obligation. Write `must` or `should` for what a person does, the present tense for what the system does, or open with `To` and the goal."
let description "Flags `needs to`, `is expected to`, `is recommended`, and other phrases that hide how strong an obligation is."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "You do not need to restart the node."
allows "The plan covers the need to scale reads."
allows "It is important to note the version."
allows "If you want to pin the version, set the tag."
allows "A timeout is required for every request."
allows "You must pin the version."
allows "You might need to restart the node."
allows "You only need to set the flag once."
allows "We just need to hand back control."
allows "You rarely need to annotate types."
allows "It is rare to need to call the method directly."
allows "You are not going to need to restart the node."
allows "Read the guide for everything you need to know."
